This directory contains the libSsrc sample-rate converter distributed with
eac3to 4.00. The original libSsrc wrapper was created by madshi.net on
2008-10-19, based on Otachan's foobar2000 plugin and Naoki Shibata's SSRC.

The resampling code is distributed under the GNU Lesser General Public License;
see COPYING. The FFT and Bessel routines are by Takuya Ooura. See the original
distribution description in README.original.txt for their attribution and terms.

Linux portability changes, 2026-10-14:
- Replace Windows headers and calling-convention declarations with portable
  equivalents; provide the integer latency helper locally.
- Move the DLL entry points out of ssrc.h into the C interface in internal/ssrc.
- Add a header guard and use the standard sizeof(type) spelling.
- Normalize source line endings and convert Japanese comments to UTF-8.
- Expose a bound derived from the filter lengths for optional tail completion.
  FinishComplete supplies zero extension through the existing processing path
  and caps the result to the input duration. Finish retains the original flush.

The filter coefficients, FFT, Bessel routine, sample processing, and buffering
algorithm are retained. Different compilers and math libraries can produce
floating-point rounding differences; Windows output equivalence needs separate
sample comparisons.

Windows amd64 portability additions:
- Keep compiler-provided calling-convention macros when they already exist.
- Save and restore each calling thread's x87 control word around initialization,
  processing and flushing. Use extended precision consistently so Windows worker
  thread defaults do not change filter coefficients or samples.
